{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And if he is poor and his means do not suffice.</b> One needs to know why the verse doubled the expression. And perhaps it intends along the lines of what they said in the last chapter of Karetot (27a), and this is the language of the Mishnah: if one set aside [money] for a lamb or for a goat, the poor person may bring a bird, etc., end of quote. And in the Gemara there they derived the matter from what the verse says concerning the offering of the fluctuating obligation (oleh veyored): ״מֵחַטָּאתוֹ אֲשֶׁר חָטָא״ — this implies, from a partial amount of the matter, that he had set [the money] aside for his sin-offering; end of quote. Now this derivation exists regarding the fluctuating offering that applies at the level of extreme poverty (dalei dalut), for there the verse mentions ״מֵחַטָּאתוֹ״ from which they derived it. But in the case of the metzora, the owner of the law could object and say: just as we do not find therein that he may bring an offering of extreme poverty, and the verse was stringent with him, so too we should be stringent with him if he set aside a lamb or a sheep for his sin-offering and his burnt-offering and they became unavailable to him — that he should not bring a partial amount of what he set aside for his sin-offering to bring in their stead two turtledoves or two young pigeons. Scripture therefore says <b>and his means do not suffice</b> — that even if he was not poor and set [the offering] aside from wealth, if his means do not suffice he may remove the designated money to profane [status] and bring the atonement that his means suffice for. And you will find that our Rabbis of blessed memory did not distinguish between the fluctuating offering of the metzora and the fluctuating offering of sanctuary defilement, etc., and a false oath, etc." "<b>Or</b> it may intend along the lines of what they said at the end of Karetot (Keritot 28a) in that [Mishnah] which I cited nearby, and this is their language: if one set aside [money] for the tenth of an ephah and the person became rich, he brings a bird; if the person became rich, he brings a sheep — end of quote, and they said there that they derived this from the verse ״עַל חַטָּאתוֹ״. And here the verse alluded to the matter by saying <b>he is poor and his means</b> etc. — meaning, his means still do not suffice, but if his means later suffice, even though he set [the offering] aside while poor, he must bring a wealthy person's offering. And these two ways can be borne together in the meaning of the verse along this line — his means still do not suffice, whether for a stringency or for a leniency as mentioned." "And in Torat Kohanim they said, and this is their language: ״poor he is״ — do I hear this means poor in the sense that he had been worth a hundred maneh and became worth fifty? Scripture says ״and his means״ etc. — or perhaps ״and his means״ etc. means he has [money] but cannot find [an animal] to purchase? Scripture says ״poor״ — end of quote. Now one needs to understand the meaning of ״cannot find״: if he cannot find at all, as in the days when Jerusalem was under siege, then certainly he brings a poor person's offering, and he is tantamount to one lacking funds — for what difference is it to me whether he has no money or he has money but it is worth nothing relative to the offering, since he cannot obtain it with money? Rather, certainly the intent of the Tanna is that [the animal] is not available through ordinary acquisition and he must exert himself greatly in money to acquire it." "<b>And it appears</b> that he is not obligated unless [the determination is made] at the threshold [point] where he would not be lacking what he needs for himself — for even if he descends [to a lower level], we say that he should bring a poor person's offering and sustain himself from what remains. And this explanation of the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory does not contradict our words, and these and those are the words of the living Elohim. And you shall know that if the Torah's intent in doubling the expression were only to state the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory, it should have written ״and if his means do not suffice for a lamb or a sheep he shall bring״ etc., as it says concerning the offering for sanctuary defilement and oaths (Leviticus 5:7); rather, it intended as well what we have explained:" "<b>And what</b> they further derived in Torat Kohanim in this parashah (Leviticus Rabbah 14:30–31) from three superfluous expressions regarding ״his means suffice״ — and they are <b>that his hand attains</b>, <b>from what his hand attains</b>, <b>et what his hand attains</b> — and they interpreted them along this line: ״that [his hand] attains״ comes to [teach] about one who was rich beforehand and at the time of bringing the offering became poor — that he too brings a poor person's offering. <b>And from what his hand attains</b> comes for one who was rich and began to bring his guilt-offering as well while still rich, and then became poor — that he too brings a poor person's offering. And <b>et what his hand attains</b> comes for one who began to bring his offering, which is the guilt-offering, at a time when he was poor, and then became rich — [teaching that] he must bring it with wealth; but if he brought a sin-offering in poverty, he does not bring a burnt-offering in wealth if he became rich. And they derived this matter there from what the verse says: <b>one for a sin-offering and one for a burnt-offering</b> — teaching that both must be of the same kind. From all these derivations one cannot learn what we derived from the doubling of the verse's saying ״poor and his means״ etc., for even after all the derivations they made, we still need the two derivations that we made: that after he set aside a wealthy person's offering he may bring a poor person's offering, and after he set aside a poor person's offering he must bring a wealthy person's offering — for these two matters did not enter into the aforementioned derivations."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:56:54.931101373Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}